Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "Play-type analysis",
    "Points per possession (PPP)",
    "Sequential effects in basketball",
    "Defensive outcomes of offensive plays",
    "Play labeling/categorization",
    "AVOID labels"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "Definition and measurement of 'AVOID' labels",
    "Attribution of lagged effects to specific play types",
    "Confounding factors in sequential play analysis",
    "Statistical methods for relating play characteristics to outcomes and labels",
    "Reliability of play-type labeling in PPP calculations"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF07",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "[APPA-049] A2 aftershock 8/8 PTs replicate all-games and playoffs (CUT +0.119 n=21,398 / PO +0.131 n=1,145 through TRANSITION +0.135 n=26,262 / +0.126 n=1,529). Top pair premiums: PUTBACK->TRANSITION +0.084 n=565; CUT->P [cut; read_family for the full text]. [APPS-037] All-games aftershock after success: CUT +0.119 (n=21398), DRIVE +0.123 (57621), PNR +0.127 (51194), POST_UP +0.128 (20619), PULL_UP +0.132 (26060), PUTBACK +0.130 (5888), SPOT_UP +0.128 (43857), TRANSITION +0. [cut; read_family for the full text]."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "OTHER: 36% end in fouls, PPP inflated (FOUL_BUCKET). DRIVE: 12pp below foul benchmark (FOUL_LEAK). PNR shows 22% foul rate. Only 335 of 50,668 possessions (0.7%) map to a real play type via initiation_type."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
      "what_failed": "Play-type PPP differences interpreted as real efficiency without accounting for labeling/foul artifacts.",
      "why": "C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw)"
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "The existence and measurability of 'defensive aftershocks,' where a team's offensive play type influences the opponent's PPP on their subsequent possession (FAM:SF07).",
    "The necessity of carefully auditing play-type labeling and PPP calculations, particularly regarding the impact of fouls and initiation type mapping, to avoid artifacts (FAM:SF-CHT-025)."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "A definition or methodology for identifying 'AVOID labels'.",
    "Whether the magnitude of observed PPP changes is 'large enough' to drive such labels, as this depends on the definition of 'AVOID labels'.",
    "Whether 'own-sequence lags' (effects on the team's own subsequent possessions) are significant or how they might contribute to 'AVOID labels'.",
    "Specific confounding factors relevant to the proposed research question beyond general labeling issues."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The research question implies that 'AVOID labels' are a derived characteristic of plays, potentially based on undesirable lagged outcomes, rather than pre-existing, independently defined labels.",
    "The concept of 'AVOID labels' will need to be operationalized as a dependent variable, likely as a binary or categorical classification, derived from observed lagged PPP metrics or other sequential outcomes."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The specific definition and derivation method for 'AVOID labels' are not provided.",
    "The exact scope and measurement of 'own-sequence lags' are not detailed.",
    "The threshold for what constitutes a 'large enough' PPP impact to drive an 'AVOID label' is undefined."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "The historical evidence confirms the existence of 'defensive aftershocks' (FAM:SF07), making the core premise of lagged PPP effects testable. However, it also highlights significant risks related to play-type labeling and PPP calculation artifacts (FAM:SF-CHT-025), which necessitates a more robust definition of 'AVOID labels' and careful methodological controls to prevent misinterpretation of results. This shifts the focus from merely finding a correlation to ensuring the labels themselves are not artifacts."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.8,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
        "text": "The definition of 'AVOID labels' is critical. If these labels are directly derived from the very PPP metrics being investigated (opponent next-possession PPP or own-sequence lags), the question risks becoming tautological or a test of a definitional threshold rather than an empirical relationship. A clear, independent or robustly derived definition is needed."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Many factors beyond the preceding play type can influence subsequent possessions, such as timeouts, substitutions, game state, player fatigue, or opponent adjustments. Without proper controls, observed correlations could be confounded by these variables."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "partly",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF07"
    ],
    "summary": "FAM:SF07 already established the existence of 'defensive aftershocks' across all 8 tested play types, meaning that the opponent's next-possession PPP is indeed influenced by the preceding offensive play. This confirms the 'opponent next-possession PPP' component of the question is a real phenomenon. However, it does not address the 'AVOID labels' aspect or 'own-sequence lags'."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "If 'AVOID labels' are defined post-hoc based on observed lagged PPP outcomes, the study will be circular. A clear, pre-defined, and ideally independently validated method for assigning 'AVOID labels' is needed."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "The sequential nature of basketball possessions means that many factors can influence subsequent outcomes. Confounders like game situation (score, clock), player personnel changes (substitutions), timeouts, or even previous fouls could affect lagged PPP metrics. A robust control strategy is essential."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "As highlighted by FAM:SF-CHT-025, play-type labeling itself can be prone to artifacts (e.g., foul leakage, misclassification). The reliability of the underlying play-type labels and the accuracy of PPP calculations, especially when considering lagged effects, must be ensured to avoid measuring an artifact."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Some specific play types or sequences might occur infrequently, leading to small sample sizes for certain lagged effects, which could limit statistical power and generalizability (as noted in FAM:SF07 for some pair premiums)."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.5,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.1,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.1
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The causal effect of running a specific offensive play type (X) on: 1) the opponent's expected points per possession (PPP) on their immediate next possession, and 2) the team's own expected PPP on its immediate next possession, and whether these lagged PPP effects are significant enough to be associated with (or to define) 'AVOID' labels for play X. The estimand is the difference in lagged PPP for a given play type X compared to a baseline or control, and the correlation/predictive power of these lagged PPPs with 'AVOID' labels.",
    "unit": "Possession sequence (Play X followed by Opponent Possession or Play X followed by Own Possession). The primary unit of analysis for lagged effects will be the pair of consecutive possessions (P_i, P_i+1) where P_i is the play of interest and P_i+1 is the subsequent possession.",
    "sample": "All NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 (complete regular season and playoffs) play-by-play possessions that can be reliably assigned a play type. Exclude possessions ending in technical fouls, end-of-quarter heaves, or other non-standard events. Filter for sufficient sample size per play type to ensure stable PPP estimates.",
    "power": "Aim for 80% power to detect a 0.05 PPP difference (half of the average aftershock observed in FAM:SF07) at alpha=0.05 for frequently occurring play types. Sample size calculations will be performed based on observed possession counts per play type. Larger effects are anticipated to be detectable for less frequent play types. The power for associating lagged PPP with AVOID labels will depend on the base rate of AVOID labels and the effect size.",
    "controls": "Statistical control for confounding variables using regression models (e.g., mixed-effects models or propensity score matching). Controls will include: game state (score differential, time remaining), quarter, team and opponent strength (e.g., ELO rating or net rating), specific players on court (using lineup data), and the outcome of the immediate preceding possession (e.g., made shot, turnover, defensive rebound) to account for momentum or structural effects. The analysis will also control for the unconditional average PPP of the subsequent play type to isolate the 'aftershock' effect.",
    "falsification": "A falsification test could involve analyzing lagged PPP for randomly shuffled play sequences to ensure that observed effects are not merely due to systemic biases in PPP calculation or general game flow. Another falsification check would be to see if lagged PPP effects are observed for non-causal sequences (e.g., after-timeout plays that reset the game state).",
    "multiplicity": "Adjust for multiple comparisons (e.g., using Bonferroni correction or FDR) if testing the lagged PPP effect for numerous individual play types simultaneously or for multiple 'AVOID label' definitions. Prioritize a small set of primary hypotheses based on the most common play types.",
    "decision_rule": "The research question will be considered 'supported' if statistically significant (p < 0.05 after multiplicity adjustment) lagged PPP effects are observed for multiple common play types, and these effects are demonstrably correlated with or predictive of independently defined 'AVOID labels'. The magnitude of the PPP changes must also exceed a pre-defined threshold (e.g., 0.05 PPP) to be considered 'large enough'. If AVOID labels are derived, the decision rule will focus on whether the derived labels align with the 'large enough' criteria and if the derivation method is robust against artifacts.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 regular season and playoffs. Play-by-play data, substitution data, box-score summaries, and derived play-type possession records from the graph database."
  }
}

